-- Theory questions for MTH 221. Safe to run twice.
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 221$q$, $q$Real Analysis I$q$, $q$Real numbers$q$, $q$Define an upper bound and the supremum of a set. State the completeness axiom of the real numbers and find the supremum of the set {1 - 1/n : n = 1, 2, 3, ...}.$q$, 5, $q$A number M is an upper bound of a set S if x is at most M for every x in S. The supremum is the least upper bound. The completeness axiom says that every non-empty set of real numbers that is bounded above has a supremum in the real numbers. For S = {1 - 1/n}, every element is less than 1, so 1 is an upper bound. If a number c is less than 1, choose n larger than 1/(1 - c), then 1 - 1/n is greater than c, so c is not an upper bound. Hence sup S = 1. Note 1 is not in the set.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definition of upper bound and supremum","m":1},{"t":"Completeness axiom","m":1},{"t":"1 is an upper bound","m":1},{"t":"No smaller number is an upper bound","m":1},{"t":"sup S = 1","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define an upper bound and the supremum of a set. State the completeness axiom of the real numbers and find the supremum of the set {1 - 1/n : n = 1, 2, 3, ...}.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 221$q$, $q$Real Analysis I$q$, $q$Sequences$q$, $q$Give the epsilon-N definition of convergence of a sequence. Use it to prove that 1/n converges to 0.$q$, 5, $q$A sequence (a_n) converges to L if for every epsilon greater than 0 there is a positive integer N such that |a_n - L| is less than epsilon for all n greater than or equal to N. For a_n = 1/n and L = 0, let epsilon be greater than 0. Choose N to be an integer larger than 1/epsilon. For n at least N, |1/n - 0| = 1/n is at most 1/N, which is less than epsilon. Hence 1/n converges to 0.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Correct epsilon-N definition","m":2},{"t":"Takes an arbitrary epsilon","m":1},{"t":"Chooses N greater than 1/epsilon","m":1},{"t":"Shows 1/n is less than epsilon for n at least N","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Give the epsilon-N definition of convergence of a sequence. Use it to prove that 1/n converges to 0.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 221$q$, $q$Real Analysis I$q$, $q$Sequences$q$, $q$Prove that every convergent sequence is bounded.$q$, 5, $q$Let (a_n) converge to L. Take epsilon = 1. Then there is N such that |a_n - L| is less than 1 for all n at least N. So |a_n| is less than |L| + 1 for those n, by the triangle inequality. The remaining terms a_1, ..., a_(N-1) are finitely many. Let M be the largest of |a_1|, ..., |a_(N-1)| and |L| + 1. Then |a_n| is at most M for every n. So the sequence is bounded.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Uses the definition with epsilon = 1","m":1},{"t":"Bounds the terms from N onward by |L| + 1","m":2},{"t":"Handles the first N - 1 terms","m":1},{"t":"Takes M as the maximum and concludes","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Prove that every convergent sequence is bounded.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 221$q$, $q$Real Analysis I$q$, $q$Monotone convergence$q$, $q$State the monotone convergence theorem. Show that the sequence defined by a_1 = 1 and a_(n+1) = square root of (2 + a_n) converges and find its limit.$q$, 7, $q$The monotone convergence theorem says that a sequence that is increasing and bounded above converges (and a decreasing sequence that is bounded below converges). Here a_1 = 1 is less than 2. If a_n is less than 2 then a_(n+1) = sqrt(2 + a_n) is less than sqrt(4) = 2, so by induction every term is less than 2. Also a_(n+1) is greater than a_n because a_(n+1)^2 - a_n^2 = 2 + a_n - a_n^2 = (2 - a_n)(1 + a_n), which is positive when a_n is between 0 and 2. So the sequence is increasing and bounded above, and it converges to a limit L. Taking limits in a_(n+1)^2 = 2 + a_n gives L^2 = 2 + L, so (L - 2)(L + 1) = 0. Since L is positive, L = 2.$q$, $q$[{"t":"States the theorem","m":1},{"t":"Shows bounded above by 2 using induction","m":2},{"t":"Shows increasing","m":2},{"t":"Equation L^2 = 2 + L","m":1},{"t":"L = 2","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$State the monotone convergence theorem. Show that the sequence defined by a_1 = 1 and a_(n+1) = square root of (2 + a_n) converges and find its limit.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 221$q$, $q$Real Analysis I$q$, $q$Series$q$, $q$State the ratio test for convergence of a series. Use it to test the series whose nth term is n/2^n.$q$, 5, $q$The ratio test: for a series of positive terms a_n let L be the limit of a_(n+1)/a_n. If L is less than 1 the series converges, if L is greater than 1 it diverges, and if L = 1 the test is inconclusive. Here a_n = n/2^n, so a_(n+1)/a_n = ((n + 1)/2^(n+1)) x (2^n/n) = (n + 1)/(2n), which tends to 1/2 as n tends to infinity. Since 1/2 is less than 1, the series converges.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Statement of the ratio test with all three cases","m":2},{"t":"Forms the ratio (n + 1)/(2n)","m":1},{"t":"Limit 1/2","m":1},{"t":"Concludes convergence","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$State the ratio test for convergence of a series. Use it to test the series whose nth term is n/2^n.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 221$q$, $q$Real Analysis I$q$, $q$Series$q$, $q$Distinguish between absolute and conditional convergence of a series. Give an example of a conditionally convergent series.$q$, 5, $q$A series converges absolutely if the series of absolute values converges. It converges conditionally if it converges but the series of absolute values diverges. Every absolutely convergent series converges. The alternating harmonic series 1 - 1/2 + 1/3 - 1/4 + ... converges by the alternating series test because the terms decrease to 0. The series of absolute values is the harmonic series 1 + 1/2 + 1/3 + ..., which diverges. So the alternating harmonic series is conditionally convergent. Rearranging a conditionally convergent series can change its sum.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definition of absolute convergence","m":1},{"t":"Definition of conditional convergence","m":1},{"t":"Correct example","m":1},{"t":"Shows it converges and the absolute series diverges","m":2}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Distinguish between absolute and conditional convergence of a series. Give an example of a conditionally convergent series.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 221$q$, $q$Real Analysis I$q$, $q$Differentiable functions$q$, $q$State Rolle's theorem. Verify it for f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3 on the interval [1, 3].$q$, 5, $q$Rolle's theorem: if f is continuous on [a, b], differentiable on (a, b) and f(a) = f(b), then there is a number c in (a, b) with f'(c) = 0. For f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3 the function is a polynomial so it is continuous and differentiable. f(1) = 1 - 4 + 3 = 0 and f(3) = 9 - 12 + 3 = 0, so f(1) = f(3). Now f'(x) = 2x - 4, which is 0 at x = 2. Since 2 is in (1, 3), the theorem is verified with c = 2.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Statement with all three conditions","m":2},{"t":"Checks continuity and differentiability","m":1},{"t":"f(1) = f(3) = 0","m":1},{"t":"c = 2 in (1, 3)","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$State Rolle's theorem. Verify it for f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3 on the interval [1, 3].$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 221$q$, $q$Real Analysis I$q$, $q$Differentiable functions$q$, $q$State the mean value theorem and find the value of c for f(x) = x^2 on [1, 3].$q$, 5, $q$The mean value theorem: if f is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b), then there is c in (a, b) with f'(c) = (f(b) - f(a))/(b - a). For f(x) = x^2 on [1, 3], f(3) = 9 and f(1) = 1, so the slope of the chord is (9 - 1)/(3 - 1) = 4. Since f'(x) = 2x, we need 2c = 4, so c = 2, which lies in (1, 3).$q$, $q$[{"t":"Correct statement","m":2},{"t":"Chord slope 4","m":1},{"t":"f'(x) = 2x and 2c = 4","m":1},{"t":"c = 2","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$State the mean value theorem and find the value of c for f(x) = x^2 on [1, 3].$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 221$q$, $q$Real Analysis I$q$, $q$Taylor series$q$, $q$Write down the Maclaurin series of e^x up to the term in x^3. Use it to estimate e^0.1.$q$, 5, $q$The Maclaurin series of e^x is 1 + x + x^2/2! + x^3/3! + ... because every derivative of e^x equals e^x, which is 1 at x = 0. Up to x^3: e^x is approximately 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6. For x = 0.1: 1 + 0.1 + 0.005 + 0.000167 = 1.105167. So e^0.1 is approximately 1.1052.$q$, $q$[{"t":"General Maclaurin series with factorials","m":2},{"t":"Reason: all derivatives equal 1 at 0","m":1},{"t":"Substitutes x = 0.1","m":1},{"t":"Approximation 1.1052","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Write down the Maclaurin series of e^x up to the term in x^3. Use it to estimate e^0.1.$q$);
select course_code, count(*) as questions from public.theory_questions group by course_code order by course_code;
